data r2_tokens_doc_as_of: Timestamp = "2026-08-18"

// THE READ ID IS THE ONE PERMISSION GROUP ID THIS MODULE PINS, AND THE WRITE ID IS DELIBERATELY NOT
// A SECOND ONE. The write half -- Cloudflare names the groups "Workers R2 Storage Read" and "Workers
// R2 Storage Edit" on its public permission reference -- has no id here: THAT PAGE PUBLISHES NO IDS
// (retrieved 2026-09-10), so there is no doc citation to make, and an uncited id would be worse than
// an absent one because it would be indistinguishable from a checked fact at every later reading.
// r2_object_write_grant below therefore takes the write group AS A PARAMETER, and the observing
// layer supplies it.
//
// IT IS RESOLVED BY OBSERVATION, AND THE OPERATION THAT RESOLVES IT IS ALREADY MODELED.
// cloudflare.AccountTokens.ListPermissionGroupsRaw answers GET
// /accounts/{account_id}/tokens/permission_groups, and its response rows carry both "id" and "name"
// (developers.cloudflare.com/api/resources/accounts/subresources/tokens/subresources/permission_groups/methods/list,
// retrieved 2026-09-10 -- the modeled path was checked against that document and agrees with it).
// So the write grant lands as an OBSERVATION recorded with its retrieval date, in the observing
// workflow layer, never as a doc-cited fact authored here.
//
// A THIRD ID IS IN CIRCULATION AND IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE ON PURPOSE. The
// operator's existing account token carries permission group 5bc3f8b21c554832afc660159ab75fa4, which
// is NOT the read id below and is not identified by any published document. Until the listing above
// is performed, nobody can say whether it is a read grant, a write grant, or the Account API Tokens
// Write grant the mint model expects of a bootstrap token -- and reshaping the model to whichever
// answer would make the plan work is the fabrication this note exists to prevent.
//
// THE SCOPE AXIS IS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT GAP. r2_bucket_item_resources builds a BUCKET-scoped
// resource map; that operator token grants at ACCOUNT scope ("com.cloudflare.api.account.<id>": "*"),
// which r2_account_resource_name can already spell and which no policy builder here consumes. Those
// are materially different grants -- one names a bucket, the other names everything in the account --
// and the difference is recorded rather than smoothed into the bucket-scoped builder.
data workers_r2_storage_bucket_item_read_permission_group_id: NonEmptyStr = "6a018a9f2fc74eb6b293b0c548f38b39"

